#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097518} :activity/id #uuid "3b059c68-9dd5-5f61-a9e4-d2768be710f4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049058} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097518 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097841 :tutorial/content "Consider a system of linear equations and its respective augmented matrix $M\\mathbin{:}$\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y+z &=6 \\\\ y-3z &=-13 \\\\ -x-y-z &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right. \\qquad M = \\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -13 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1 & -4\\end{array} \\right]\n$$\n\n\n\nWe can reduce $M$ to row echelon form using the standard Gaussian elimination algorithm. The algorithm that we use is very similar to the case of $2 \\times 2$ systems. \n\nOur goal is to create a descending \"staircase\" of $1$'s with $0$'s below, and we achieve this using elementary row operations. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -13 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1 & -4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -13 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -13 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6 & -24\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :=-\\frac{1}{6}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}{\\color{red}1} & -1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{red}1} & -3 & -13 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{red}1} & 4\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix is now in row echelon form, and we can apply back substitution to solve the original system of equations.\n\n**Note:** The row echelon form of a matrix is *not* unique.  In the previous example, after the second row operation we arrived at\n\n\n\n$$\n\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc|c}{\\color{red}1} & -1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{red}1} & -3 & -13 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{red}-6} & -24\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\n\n\nThis augmented matrix is already in row echelon form! However, it is always useful to do an extra step and obtain $1$'s along the main diagonal, like we did previously:\n\n\n\n$$\n\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc|c}{\\color{red}1} & -1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{red}1} & -3 & -13 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{red}1} & 4\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\n\n\nHaving $1$'s along the main diagonal makes the final back substitution process a little easier." :tutorial/id #uuid "5d8cb1c1-9e09-560f-83ad-7c8a3d0a78cc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3155 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "54dae6c6-bb01-588b-b012-dc097a5b2c65" :step/math-academy-id 17196 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097519}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097519 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097842 :tutorial/content "The **standard Gaussian elimination** for a $3 \\times 3$ system can be described as follows:\n\n1. If $a_{11} \\neq 0$ and $a_{11} \\neq 1,$ then we use the row operation $R_1:=R_1/a_{11}.$\n\n2. We use first row and the necessary row operations to make the remaining entries of the first column below $a_{11}$ equal to $0.$ Namely,\n\n   - if $a_{21} \\neq 0,$ then we use the row operation $R_2:=R_2 + (-a_{21})R_1;$\n   - if $a_{31} \\neq 0,$ then we use the row operation $R_3:=R_3 + (-a_{31})R_1.$\n\n3. If $a_{22} \\neq 1,$ then we use the row operation $R_2:=R_2/a_{22}.$\n\n4. We use the second row and the necessary row operation to make the entry below $a_{22}$ equal to $0.$ Namely,\n\n   - if $a_{32} \\neq 0,$ then we use the row operation $R_3:=R_3 + (-a_{32})R_2.$\n\n5. If $a_{33} \\neq 1,$ then we use the row operation $R_3:=R_3/a_{33}.$\n\n6. In some exceptional cases, such as when the element on the diagonal is equal to $0,$ we first need to exchange rows. Then proceed using the same steps as above.\n\nCarrying out the above algorithm will convert the matrix to row echelon form." :tutorial/id #uuid "955c2b9a-3d5f-52c3-b4f3-62541732ffcc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3156 :tutorial/title "General Algorithm"} :step/id #uuid "4f927453-918f-5476-ba92-06cc1a2e3f72" :step/math-academy-id 17197 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097520}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097520 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096672 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096459 :question/id #uuid "b77c065c-3821-513c-9376-6e7108074616" :question/math-academy-id "e-4701" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y+z &=1 \\\\ y+z &=-2 \\\\ -x+2y+z &=-1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 & -1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y+z &=1 \\\\ y+z &=-2 \\\\ z &=2,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The final equation $z=2$ tells us the value of $z.$ Substituting this value into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+(2) &=-2\\,⟹\\,y=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting both $y=-4$ and $z=2$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-(-4)+(2) &=1\\,⟹\\,x=-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-5,$ $y=-4,$ and $z=2.$ Or, in vector form, $\\langle -5,-4,2 \\rangle.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "739b1cbc-c1a7-547d-a9fb-1f244bd36827" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049422}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186757165} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096937} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097183} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266014} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287997} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287998} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367935} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368702} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395769} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186640047} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186757131} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186757132} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186757141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186757146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186757156} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186757210} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186757216} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186757220} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186757221} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186757224} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186760437} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186760446} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186760700} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186760702} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186760707} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186760709} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186760711}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 3x3 System of Equations Using Standard Gaussian Elimination"} :step/id #uuid "8c5555a5-f06e-5c31-9de6-ec1aef27abbb" :step/math-academy-id 17198 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097521}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097521 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096673 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096460 :question/id #uuid "7760b6ce-91fb-5ba6-beb2-c244d6a73d69" :question/math-academy-id "e-4702" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y+z &=-2 \\\\ x+y+2z &=-1 \\\\ x+2y+z &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n\n\n$$\nM= \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\n\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we get $a_{22}=0.$ Therefore, we swap the $2$nd and $3$rd rows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y+z &=-2 \\\\ y &=3 \\\\ z &=1,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The third equation $z=1$ tells us the value of $z,$ and the second equation $y=3$ tells us the value of $y.$ Substituting these values into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+(3)+(1) &=-2\\,⟹\\,x=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-6,$ $y=3,$ and $z=1.$ Or, in vector form, $\\langle -6,3,1 \\rangle.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd47a43f-1e99-5909-aa48-da8690025197" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186757033} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097349} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097350} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266015} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342020} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342022} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342023} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368709} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400513} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403654} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414031} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186639976} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186756998} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186757009} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186757013} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186757014} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186757021} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186757037} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186757044} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186757049} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186757064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186757065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186757070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186757072} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186757085} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186757090} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186757102} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186760465} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186760476}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 3x3 System of Equations Using Standard Gaussian Elimination with Row Exchanges"} :step/id #uuid "813ae7dd-cdf7-5b02-ad5e-3911c5f08ece" :step/math-academy-id 17199}] :activity/title "Solving 3x3 Systems of Equations Using Gaussian Elimination" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096856}]}
